<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cathedral of Saint Tryphon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1166</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Kotor Cathedral</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cathedral of Saint Tryphon is the Roman Catholic cathedral of Kotor in Montenegro, consecrated in 1166 and dedicated to the city's patron saint, whose relics it holds. A Romanesque church with two west towers, it was rebuilt after earthquakes in 1667 and 1979. It stood at the centre of the self-governing commune and of Kotor's Venetian-era life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cathedral-of-saint-tryphon.jpg" alt="Cathedral of Saint Tryphon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geotiger18 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kotor_Cathedral_church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
